Nina and I arrive at the beach.
The beach is calm, with just a few groups on the sand.
We look for Eva and Peter.
I spot them down by the water.
Peter waves to us.
"We arrived twenty minutes ago," he says.
"But we have not lit our bonfire yet," Eva adds.
We stand together and look at the pile of driftwood.
Peter lights our bonfire.
The flames quickly become very tall.
The fire warms us in the cold wind from the sea.
"The fire has already chased the chill away," Eva notes.
She opens a small punnet of fresh strawberries.
"I bought them at the market this morning," she explains.
"And I have washed them," she says with a smile.
I take a red strawberry and eat it.
It tastes like summer.
I have never experienced a summer evening like this before.
It is the perfect way to turn thirty-two.
